course: dictionary
lesson: 5620
title: Words that describe people
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A describing word can tell you what someone is like. Likeable means easy to like; pleasant and friendly. You can make it stronger with more likeable and most likeable.
explain: Writers choose words like likeable so readers feel something about a character. A likeable hero is someone we want to see succeed, so we care what happens to them. Look for clues in what the character says and does.
explain: «Maybe I'm just not likeable any more.» The speaker worries that people no longer find them pleasant and friendly. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try pleasant in its place and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Likeable means easy to like; pleasant and friendly.
fact: Likeable is an adjective, so it describes people, such as «a likeable new pupil».
fact: The forms are likeable, more likeable and most likeable.
fact: Writers make characters likeable so readers care about them.
fact: Pleasant and friendly are close in meaning to likeable, but likeable is about how easy someone is to like.
exercise: «Maybe I'm just not likeable any more.» What does likeable mean here? | Very tall and strong | Hard to find in a crowd | *Pleasant and friendly, easy to like | Always tired
exercise: Why do authors make a character likeable? | So the character is hard to find | *So readers care about them | So readers stop reading the story | So the story has no ending
exercise: Which form means the most easy to like? | *most likeable | unlikeable | more likeable | likeableness
test: «A likeable new pupil» is a pupil who is what? | Very loud | *Pleasant and friendly | Hard to understand | Always late
test: Which word is closest in meaning to likeable? | *Friendly | Grumpy | Gloomy | Cruel
exam: A story hero is described as likeable. What does that tell you about the hero? | *Readers will find them pleasant and easy to like | They are invisible to everyone | Readers should dislike them | They never speak in the story
